<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/snow-roof.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/snow-roof-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/snow-roof.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="770" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/property-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Snow Load &amp; Ice Dam Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Snow Load Damage Appraisal After Heavy Idaho Winters</h1><p class="lede">A wet, heavy snowpack followed by rain can push a roof past what it was built to carry. When trusses crack or a barn comes down, we value the structural repair as well as the finishes.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-w" href="/schedule-a-consult/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Season</dt><dd>December to March</dd></div><div><dt>Buildings</dt><dd>Homes, barns, carports</dd></div><div><dt>Key issue</dt><dd>Sudden vs. gradual</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Snow weight is a real Idaho peril</h2>
<p>Snow loads vary enormously across the state, from the comparatively mild Lewiston area to the Wood River Valley and the Panhandle, where buildings are designed for much heavier loads. Trouble starts when a winter delivers more than usual or when rain soaks an existing snowpack. The winter of 2016–17 is the example people in southwest Idaho remember: repeated heavy snow and freeze-thaw cycles led to widely reported roof and outbuilding collapses, including agricultural storage buildings in the Payette and Weiser area.</p>
<h2>What snow-load claims involve</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Structural damage</strong>: cracked or deflected trusses and rafters, bowed ridges, sheared connections and failed carports and patio covers.</li>
<li><strong>Full or partial collapse</strong> of barns, pole buildings, shops and hay sheds.</li>
<li><strong>Ice dams</strong> at the eaves that force meltwater under shingles and into walls and ceilings.</li>
<li><strong>Interior damage</strong> from leaks and from movement in the framing.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why these claims get contested</h2>
<p>Insurers often ask whether a failure was sudden or the result of long-term deterioration, rot or inadequate original construction. Many policies cover collapse caused by the weight of snow or ice but exclude gradual decay, and collapse coverage often sits in its own section with specific conditions. Those coverage questions come first.</p>
<p>Once coverage is accepted, the disputes move to scope: how many trusses need replacement rather than sistering, whether an engineer’s repair design is required, and whether the roof covering has to be replaced to reach the framing. Snow load damage appraisal resolves those amount questions.</p>
<h2>How we document it</h2>
<p>We photograph framing from the attic or interior, record deflection and cracking, review any engineer’s report and price the repair method the engineer or code official requires. Our <a href="/policy-math-tools/roof-snow-load-estimator/">roof snow load estimator</a> helps you understand the weight on your roof during a heavy winter.</p>

<h2>Questions about snow load damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a barn that collapsed under snow covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Farm and homeowners policies often cover collapse from the weight of snow or ice, but outbuildings may be insured on a different basis or excluded. Read the farm structures or other-structures section of your policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I shovel my roof?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Removing heavy snow can reduce the risk of damage, but roof work in winter is dangerous. Hire insured professionals, and never stand on a roof that is already sagging.</p></div></details><details><summary>The insurer says the trusses were undersized. What then?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>That’s a causation and coverage argument. An engineer’s opinion is often needed. If coverage is accepted and only the repair is disputed, appraisal can settle the amount.</p></div></details></div>
